{"chunk_id": "3ad3d1079f25", "text": "This section explains the Sankhya distinction between Prakriti (material nature) and Purusha (pure consciousness). Through a series of analogies\u2014reed and sheath, worm and fruit, fish and water, fire and pot, lotus leaf and water\u2014the text emphasizes that two things may coexist intimately yet remain fundamentally separate. Misunderstanding this relationship leads to spiritual ignorance. Yajnavalkya then turns to Yoga, declaring that Sankhya and Yoga ultimately pursue the same goal: liberation. Yoga works through discipline of breath and senses, progressing from concentration with attributes to a higher state beyond distinctions. By withdrawing the senses and fixing the mind step by step toward the Supreme, the yogin reaches samadhi\u2014an unmoving, lamp\u2011like stillness in which the radiant Brahman is realized, leading eventually to emancipation.\nYajnavalkya explains that Purusha is distinct from Prakriti and teaches Yoga practices leading to liberation, including breath control, sense withdrawal, and Samadhi.\nThemes: Purusha\u2013Prakriti distinction, Sankhya and Yoga unity, breath control and meditation, Samadhi, path to liberation", "vector": [0.03047798201441765, 0.009886888787150383, 0.010684431530535221, 0.009036616422235966, -0.03282447159290314, 0.006555007304996252, -0.025574086233973503, -0.019734229892492294, -0.02372853271663189, -0.0259300135076046, 0.014092111960053444, 0.025429077446460724, 0.009550734423100948, 0.026865972205996513, 0.0005998045671731234, -0.0034307504538446665, 0.04624427482485771, -0.01143583469092846, 0.02813149429857731, -0.007652451749891043, -0.0038888428825885057, -0.006676945369690657, 0.002100963843986392, -0.001371805788949132, -0.006907639559358358, 0.008838878944516182, 0.025692729279398918, -0.018007319420576096, -0.009313449263572693, -0.011771989054977894, -0.008206117898225784, -0.0012885911855846643, -0.014619412831962109, 0.004897305741906166, -0.012239968404173851